      "heading": "Why beam search backfires here",
      "narration": "Beam search deserves its own slide because it is the strategy that sounds most obviously correct and is most obviously wrong here, and understanding why teaches you something about the whole enterprise. The method is sensible. Greedy is myopic: it commits to the best token now even if that forecloses a much better sentence later. So keep several options alive. At each step, maintain the b most probable partial sequences — b is the beam width, usually four or five — extend all of them, keep the best b of the extensions, and at the end return the complete sequence with the highest total probability. It is a principled search for the most likely output, and for tasks where a single correct output exists it genuinely helps. Translation and summarization both use it, and it beats sampling there. Now point it at open-ended generation — a story, an explanation, a chat reply — and the output is noticeably bad. Bland, short, repetitive, and strangely lifeless. And here is the part I want you to sit with: that is not a failure of the search. The search is working perfectly. It is finding a very high probability sequence, which is exactly what you asked it for. The problem is that the request was wrong. The most probable sequence is not the best sequence, and once you say that out loud it is obvious. Look at how humans actually write. Real text is full of choices that were not the single likeliest continuation — a specific example instead of a generic one, an unexpected verb, a turn the reader did not see coming. That surprisingness is not noise on top of good writing; it is a large part of what makes writing good. A model maximizing total sequence probability is explicitly avoiding all of it. What you get is the average of everything the model has ever read, which is the most predictable text it is capable of producing. There is a well-known measurement of this: human-written text sits at a fairly consistent level of per-token surprise, and beam search output sits far below it. It is too likely to be human. There is a second, more mechanical problem. Every token you add multiplies the sequence probability by a number less than one, so longer sequences are always less probable than shorter ones. Beam search therefore has a systematic bias toward stopping early, and implementations patch this with a length normalization or a length penalty. That patch works, and notice that it is a patch — you are hand-correcting an objective that does not match what you want. So the rule is simple, and the reasoning behind it generalizes. Use beam search when there is one right answer and you want the most likely one: translation, summarization, structured extraction, anything with a reference output. Use sampling with top-p when there are many acceptable answers and you want a good one rather than the modal one. And carry the deeper point out of this lesson: likelihood was the training objective because it was trainable, not because high likelihood is what makes text good. Those are different things, and every generation strategy is a way of negotiating the gap.",
