      "heading": "The KV cache, precisely",
      "narration": "You met this problem when you wrote generate in the Mini-GPT lesson, and now here is the fix. Recall the waste. At every step, the naive loop feeds the entire sequence through the model again, which recomputes the keys and values for every token already processed. Now ask whether any of those could have changed. They could not — and the reason is precise. The causal mask guarantees that a token attends only to itself and to earlier positions, so a token's representation, and therefore its key and its value, depends on nothing that comes after it. Once computed, they are fixed for the rest of the generation. Recomputing them is not merely inefficient; it is provably recomputing an identical number. So cache them. Keep, for each layer and each head, the keys and values you have already produced. At each new step, you run the model on exactly one token: compute its query, key and value, attend that single query against the whole cached set of keys and values, and append the new key and value to the cache. The complexity of generating n tokens drops from order n cubed to order n squared, and — the thing you feel — the cost per token stops growing with the length of what you have already written. This changes the shape of inference in a way you need to know about, because it explains the latency behaviour of every system you will build on. There are now two distinct phases. Prefill processes the whole prompt at once, in parallel, in a single forward pass. It is compute-bound, it uses the hardware efficiently, and it is what determines your time to first token. Then decode runs one token at a time. Each step does very little arithmetic but must read the entire model's weights and the entire cache from memory, so it is memory-bandwidth-bound, and it is what determines your tokens per second. These two phases want different optimizations and are measured separately, which is why serving benchmarks always report both numbers. Now the cost, because there is one and it is the binding constraint in production. The cache holds two tensors — keys and values — per layer, per head, per position. Multiply it out: two, times the number of layers, times the number of heads, times the head dimension, times the sequence length, times the batch size. For a large model at long context this reaches many gigabytes per sequence. And that is per user: every concurrent conversation needs its own cache. So on a served endpoint, it is not the model weights that limit how many people you can handle at once — the weights are loaded once and shared. It is the cache. This is why long context is expensive in a way that surprises people, and it is the direct motivation for multi-query and grouped-query attention, where several attention heads share one set of keys and values specifically to shrink this number.",
