      "heading": "Shrinking, batching, and matching strategy to task",
      "narration": "Three pieces of engineering and then a practical table. Quantization first, and note that this is different from the QLoRA case: there you quantized a base model so you could train an adapter on top, here you are shrinking a finished model purely to serve it. Store the weights in eight bits or four instead of sixteen. Eight-bit is essentially free — the quality difference is not measurable on most tasks. Four-bit, using GPTQ or AWQ or the GGUF formats that run on consumer hardware, costs a little quality and quarters your memory, which is often the difference between a model fitting on your card and not. And there is a second benefit that people miss: it makes generation faster, and understanding why matters. Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, which means the accelerator spends most of its time waiting to read weights, not computing with them. Four-bit weights are a quarter of the bytes to move, so you wait a quarter as long. Fewer bytes is the speedup. Second, continuous batching, which is the single largest throughput win in serving and is purely a scheduling idea. Naive batching groups requests, runs them together, and waits for all of them to finish before starting the next batch — so one long generation holds up a batch full of short ones, and the accelerator idles on the slots that finished early. Continuous batching, sometimes called in-flight batching, operates at the level of a single decoding step: after each step, any sequence that has finished is retired and a waiting request takes its slot immediately. The hardware stays full. This routinely multiplies throughput several times over with no effect on output quality at all, which is a rare kind of improvement. Third, PagedAttention, from vLLM. The problem it solves: because you do not know how long a generation will be, the naive implementation reserves a contiguous block of cache memory for the maximum possible length of every request, and most of it is never used. That is severe fragmentation. PagedAttention borrows the idea of virtual memory: store the cache in fixed-size blocks that need not be contiguous, and allocate them on demand. Much higher memory utilization, therefore many more concurrent requests on the same hardware, and it makes sharing a common prompt prefix across requests almost free as a side effect. Finally, the practical table, which is the thing to actually take away. For deterministic extraction — pulling fields out of a document, classification, anything you will parse — use temperature zero and check that your provider honours it. For code, around zero point two: code needs to be correct more than it needs to be interesting. For prose, explanation and chat, zero point seven to zero point eight with top-p at zero point nine. For brainstorming, where you want range and will filter afterwards, one point zero or above. And add a repetition penalty around one point one when you see loops, though if you need much more than that the real problem is usually your prompt. One last piece of advice that will save you an afternoon: log your decoding settings alongside your outputs. When somebody reports that the model gave a different answer yesterday, the cause is almost never the model. It is a temperature that nobody wrote down.",
